When code branches — `if / else` — Big-O analysis takes the **most expensive branch**, never an average of the two.

```python
def process(arr, target):
    if target in arr:       # O(n) scan
        return True
    else:
        return sort_and_check(arr, target)  # O(n log n)
```

The `if` branch is O(n); the `else` branch is O(n log n). The function's worst-case complexity is **O(n log n)** — the slower branch — because Big-O by convention describes the guarantee across all inputs, and some inputs will take that branch.

This is a direct application of "worst case" from `best-worst-average-case`: a conditional is exactly the code shape that produces different cases in the first place. Reporting the cheaper branch's cost as *the* complexity is a common way candidates understate their own solution's worst case.
